{
  "title": "Managerial Decision-Making",
  "slides": [
    {
      "title": "Learning Objectives",
      "points": [
        {
          "text": "Describe basic characteristics of managerial decision-making.",
          "sources": ["objectives.md#Learning objectives"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Contrast reflective and reactive decision-making systems.",
          "sources": ["objectives.md#Learning objectives"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Distinguish programmed from nonprogrammed decisions.",
          "sources": ["objectives.md#Learning objectives"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Identify barriers to effective decision-making, including confirmation bias.",
          "sources": ["objectives.md#Learning objectives"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Apply techniques that improve individual and group decisions.",
          "sources": ["objectives.md#Learning objectives"]
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        {
          "text": "Present these as the main questions this session will answer.",
          "sources": ["objectives.md#Learning objectives", "openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p1"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Ask which objective students feel least confident about.",
          "sources": ["objectives.md#Learning objectives"]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Why Managerial Decision-Making Matters",
      "points": [
        {
          "text": "Managers make decisions on a daily basis.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p3", "openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p25"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Decision-making is thinking through options and selecting one.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p3", "openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p25"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Decision quality affects the organization and its stakeholders.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p3", "openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p25"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Top managers decide about technology or product lines; lower-level managers affect departments.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p3"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Stakeholders include owners, employees, customers, suppliers, and community members.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p25"]
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        {
          "text": "Use the Away founders example to show that even starting a business is a sequence of decisions.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p1", "openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p3"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Ask why a scheduling decision can matter even if it does not determine firm survival.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p3"]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Basic Characteristics",
      "points": [
        {
          "text": "Managerial decisions often have significant impact and implications.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p25"]
        },
        {
          "text": "They often involve complexity, incomplete information, and time constraints.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p25", "openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p3", "openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p10"]
        },
        {
          "text": "There is rarely one right answer.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p25", "openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p4"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Sometimes there are multiple good options or multiple bad options.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p25", "openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p4"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Managers must weigh consequences and recognize conflicting stakeholder needs.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p25"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Some decisions have ethical implications.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p25", "openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p4"]
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        {
          "text": "Ask what is wrong with treating management decisions like multiple-choice tests.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p4"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Pitfall: students may think managers should simply wait for perfect information; waiting too long can also be harmful.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p3"]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Two Brain Systems",
      "points": [
        {
          "text": "The reflective system is logical, analytical, and methodical.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p25"]
        },
        {
          "text": "The reactive system is quick and intuitive.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p25"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Nonprogrammed decisions generally need reflective processing.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p9"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Programmed decisions can use reactive processing through heuristics.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p9", "openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p7"]
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        {
          "text": "Ask students to label one recent decision as reflective and one as reactive.",
          "sources": ["objectives.md#Learning objectives", "openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p25"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Pitfall: students may say the reactive system is always worse; it can help with routine decisions.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p9", "openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p7"]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Programmed Decisions",
      "points": [
        {
          "text": "Programmed decisions are repeated over time.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p7", "openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p25"]
        },
        {
          "text": "They can be guided by existing rules or criteria.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p7", "publisher-slides-standin.pptx#slide4"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Their criteria may be simple or fairly complex.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p7"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Example: scheduling part-time employees using anticipated business and availability.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p7"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Example: ordering raw materials based on production, existing stock, and delivery timing.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p7"]
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        {
          "text": "Give one workplace example of a programmed decision and ask students to identify the rule.",
          "sources": ["publisher-slides-standin.pptx#slide4", "openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p7"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Pitfall: students may assume programmed decisions are always simple.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p7"]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Nonprogrammed Decisions",
      "points": [
        {
          "text": "Nonprogrammed decisions are novel.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p25", "publisher-slides-standin.pptx#slide4"]
        },
        {
          "text": "They lack well-defined or known criteria.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p25", "publisher-slides-standin.pptx#slide4"]
        },
        {
          "text": "They require research, information, and other people's ideas.",
          "sources": ["publisher-slides-standin.pptx#slide4"]
        },
        {
          "text": "They should be handled with a systematic process.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p14"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Example: deciding whether to pursue a new technology or product line.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p3"]
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        {
          "text": "Ask why heuristics save time for programmed decisions but may hurt important nonprogrammed decisions.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p9", "openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p10", "openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p7"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Use the Away founder case as a nonprogrammed decision example.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p1", "openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p7", "openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p3"]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Decision-Making Process",
      "points": [
        {
          "text": "Step 1: Recognize that a decision needs to be made.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p14"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Step 2: Generate multiple alternatives.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p14"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Step 3: Analyze the alternatives.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p14"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Step 4: Select an alternative.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p14"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Step 5: Implement the selected alternative.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p14"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Step 6: Evaluate its effectiveness.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p14"]
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        {
          "text": "Ask which step is easiest to rush and what quality may be lost.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p14", "openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p15"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Pitfall: ignoring problems because they are unclear leads to bigger problems.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p15"]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Heuristics and Satisficing",
      "points": [
        {
          "text": "Heuristics are mental shortcuts based on prior experience.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p25", "openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p7", "openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p14"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Heuristics help managers reach good decisions quickly.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p25", "openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p14"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Satisficing selects the first acceptable solution.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p25", "openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p14"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Satisficing minimizes time spent on a decision.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p25"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Example: choosing generic products quickly when they are cheap enough.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p14"]
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        {
          "text": "Ask students to name one low-risk managerial decision where satisficing is acceptable.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p14"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Pitfall: time pressure can push managers toward heuristics when deep processing is needed.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p10"]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Barriers to Effective Decisions",
      "points": [
        {
          "text": "Bounded rationality limits full rationality because information and processing are incomplete.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p25", "openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p9"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Escalation of commitment keeps decision makers invested in poor decisions despite negative outcomes.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p25", "openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p10"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Time constraints can prevent enough information collection and deep processing.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p10", "openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p25"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Uncertainty means outcomes may be unknown until a choice is made.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p10"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Personal biases favor familiar people, things, and ideas.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p10"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Confirmation bias favors information that confirms existing beliefs.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p25", "objectives.md#Learning objectives"]
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        {
          "text": "Ask students to distinguish incomplete information from cognitive limits in bounded rationality.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p9", "openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p25"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Watch for students treating escalation of commitment as mere stubbornness rather than continued investment in a poor choice.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p10"]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Improving Individual Decisions",
      "points": [
        {
          "text": "Be creative to generate more options.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p14", "openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p15"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Conduct research and use evidence-based decision-making.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p14", "openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p15"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Evidence-based decision-making relies on best available evidence and data.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p15", "openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p25"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Engage in critical thinking.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p14"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Think about long-term implications.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p14"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Consider ethical implications.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p14", "openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p4"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Talk to other people, especially mentors, to generate alternatives.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p15"]
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        {
          "text": "Use the Away founders' research to illustrate evidence-based decision-making.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p1", "openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p15"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Pitfall: managers may generate only two alternatives and rush to analysis.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p15"]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Common Logical Fallacies",
      "points": [
        {
          "text": "Non sequitur: the conclusion does not logically follow from the argument.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p17"]
        },
        {
          "text": "For non sequiturs, examine arguments, assumptions, and supporting evidence.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p17"]
        },
        {
          "text": "False cause: assuming that because two things are related, one caused the other.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p17"]
        },
        {
          "text": "For false cause, ask whether another factor may explain the relationship.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p17"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Ad hominem: attacking the person rather than the argument.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p17"]
        },
        {
          "text": "For ad hominem, ask whether the argument would be persuasive if stated by someone else.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p17"]
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        {
          "text": "Ask students to find the unspoken assumption in the competitor marketing example.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p17"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Pitfall: dismissing an argument because of who said it rather than because of its reasoning.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p17"]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Advantages of Group Decisions",
      "points": [
        {
          "text": "Groups can incorporate different perspectives and ideas.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p22"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Diverse groups bring different preferences, opinions, biases, and stereotypes.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p22"]
        },
        {
          "text": "More people often bring unique information and knowledge.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p22"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Group decision-making often generates more options.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p22", "openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p24"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Brainstorming generates as many solutions or options as possible.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p22", "openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p25"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Affected people may have greater understanding and commitment.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p22"]
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        {
          "text": "Ask why a hiring committee with both men and women may reduce similarity bias.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p22"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Pitfall: students may assume that more people automatically means better decisions without diversity of perspective.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p22"]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Disadvantages of Group Decisions",
      "points": [
        {
          "text": "Groups can get bogged down by conflict.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p22"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Some groups push for agreement at the expense of quality discussion.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p22"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Groupthink occurs when members withhold concerns to keep peace.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p22", "openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p25"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Groupthink can arise from team spirit and fear of disrupting camaraderie.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p22"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Suppression of dissent occurs when a member uses power to prevent others from speaking.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p25"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Process conflict is task-oriented and constructive.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p25"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Relationship conflict is personality-based and tends to be destructive.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p25"]
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        {
          "text": "Ask students to recall a group that chose quick agreement over substantive discussion.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p22", "openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p25"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Distinguish process conflict from relationship conflict so students do not label all disagreement as harmful.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p25"]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Improving Group Decisions",
      "points": [
        {
          "text": "Have diverse members in the group.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p24"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Diversity can generate more options and reduce bias.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p24"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Assign a devil's advocate.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p24"]
        },
        {
          "text": "A devil's advocate critiques group ideas to discourage groupthink.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p25", "openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p24"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Encourage everyone to speak up and contribute.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p24"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Help group members find common ground.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p24"]
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        {
          "text": "Ask which technique directly counters suppression of dissent.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p24", "openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p25"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Pitfall: a devil's advocate may be treated as disloyal unless the role is framed as improving the decision.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p24", "openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p25"]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Conclusion",
      "points": [
        {
          "text": "Decision-making is a crucial daily activity for managers.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p25"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Decisions range from small and simple to big and complex.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p25", "openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p3"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Expertise develops through learning and experience.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p25", "openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p14"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Managers should collect information, notice biases, consider ethics, and think critically.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p25"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Effective decisions benefit the organization and its stakeholders.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p25"]
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        {
          "text": "Close by revisiting the learning objectives and ask students which barrier or technique they will apply first.",
          "sources": ["objectives.md#Learning objectives", "openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p25"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Watch for students leaving with a simple rule that fast decisions are always risky or slow decisions are always better.",
          "sources": ["openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p3", "openstax-ch2-managerial-decision-making.pdf#p14"]
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
